WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.440
INTRO:
If you’re a billionaire,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:01.440 --> 00:00:07.440
most people would agree that you’re too big to&nbsp;
fail. Even if your assets plummeted 80%, you’d&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.440 --> 00:00:11.760
be left with hundreds of millions of dollars.&nbsp;
If you were originally a multi billionaire,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:16.240
you may still even have a billion dollars left.&nbsp;
While this is true for cash billionaires with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.240 --> 00:00:20.640
little debt, not all billionaires’ fortunes&nbsp;
are built upon such solid foundations.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:25.760
In many cases, a recession or downturn in the&nbsp;
market is all that’s needed to wipe them out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.760 --> 00:00:31.840
and no one knows this better than Sean Quinn.&nbsp;
In 2008, Sean was the richest man in Ireland&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.840 --> 00:00:39.280
worth an estimated $6 billion. But by 2011, Sean&nbsp;
had not only lost it all, but he was bankrupt and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.280 --> 00:00:45.440
owed an eyewatering $3.5 billion. To this day,&nbsp;
Sean hasn’t been able to climb out of this hole,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.440 --> 00:00:50.080
and his once seemingly indestructible&nbsp;
fortune is just a blip in history. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.080 --> 00:00:57.840
here’s how Sean Quinn went from being Ireland’s&nbsp;
richest man to filing for bankruptcy in 3 years.

00:00:57.840 --> 00:01:00.880
RAGS TO RICHES:
Taking a look back,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.880 --> 00:01:06.400
Sean was born on December 5, 1947 in a small&nbsp;
village called Derrylin in Northern Ireland.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.400 --> 00:01:10.720
Sean’s family was extremely poor farmers who&nbsp;
were just trying to survive on a daily basis.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.280 --> 00:01:16.320
So, Sean never had the luxury of focusing&nbsp;
on education or building a career. He had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:20.960
to spend all his time helping run the farm, and he&nbsp;
would even drop out of school when he was just 14&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.960 --> 00:01:25.840
unable to read or write. After he dropped out,&nbsp;
Sean searched for alternative ways that he could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.840 --> 00:01:30.080
make the family farm profitable as their farm had&nbsp;
never been very lucrative when it came to growing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.080 --> 00:01:35.440
crops and livestock. It took a bit of time to come&nbsp;
up with a solution, but Sean eventually realized&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.440 --> 00:01:40.160
that his farm was filled with one substance that&nbsp;
was in high demand. And no, we’re not talking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.160 --> 00:01:46.160
about gold or drugs, we’re actually talking about&nbsp;
gravel. Sean borrowed £100 and started extracting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.160 --> 00:01:51.680
gravel from his 23 acre family farm. He would then&nbsp;
wash the gravel and sell it to local builders.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.680 --> 00:01:56.240
Selling gravel wasn’t particularly lucrative,&nbsp;
but Sean had plenty of gravel and it was a very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.240 --> 00:02:01.120
cheap business to run. Soon enough, Sean made his&nbsp;
business official by registering it as Sean Quinn&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.120 --> 00:02:05.840
Quarries Limited. Overtime, he expanded into&nbsp;
selling other construction materials as well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.840 --> 00:02:09.760
but by far his biggest jump forward was when&nbsp;
he started selling cement which would become&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.760 --> 00:02:14.720
the basis of his fortune. As the cement business&nbsp;
started to pick up steam, Sean entered some more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.720 --> 00:02:18.640
difficult industries such as glass and&nbsp;
plastics. While Sean opened up loads of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.640 --> 00:02:24.720
subsidiaries throughout the 1970s and 80s, all of&nbsp;
them were concentrated on construction. You see,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.720 --> 00:02:28.560
Sean was able to build up a substantial number of&nbsp;
construction contracts through the Gaelic Athletic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.560 --> 00:02:33.920
Association which is a group focused on promoting&nbsp;
Irish sporting and culture. Sean felt comfortable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.920 --> 00:02:38.000
dealing with members of this group, and for&nbsp;
decades, he avoided venturing into anything&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:43.040
else. But eventually in the 1990s, Sean would&nbsp;
take his first steps outside of construction.

00:02:43.040 --> 00:02:46.720
HUMBLE BILLIONAIRE:&nbsp;

00:02:46.720 --> 00:02:50.960
Sean didn’t just jump into starting a tech&nbsp;
company or investing into crypto though. In terms&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.960 --> 00:02:55.200
of diversification, Sean looked for industries&nbsp;
that had similar stability and consistency to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.200 --> 00:03:00.880
construction which led him to finance. In 1996,&nbsp;
Sean founded Quinn Financial Services which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.880 --> 00:03:06.560
offered a variety of different financial services,&nbsp;
but their most popular business was insurance.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.560 --> 00:03:10.880
This probably had to do with the fact that Sean&nbsp;
was extremely popular within Northern Ireland.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.880 --> 00:03:15.200
Though his wealth had skyrocketed from 0 to&nbsp;
hundreds of millions over the past few decades,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.200 --> 00:03:18.880
to most people who actually knew Sean, he&nbsp;
was just another hard working Irish man.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.440 --> 00:03:23.760
Sure, he had a nice house and cars, but he&nbsp;
never lost his connection to the everyday man.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.320 --> 00:03:28.320
In fact, he was constantly building up his&nbsp;
relationship with his community. Instead&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.320 --> 00:03:32.800
of bringing in highly educated people from the US&nbsp;
and Europe, Sean preferred to employ as many local&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.800 --> 00:03:38.640
people as he could. At his peak, Sean employed&nbsp;
8000 people, 5500 of which were from Ireland.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.640 --> 00:03:42.640
Given Sean’s strong connection with the community,&nbsp;
many Irish people far preferred insuring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.640 --> 00:03:47.360
themselves with Quinn Financial Services as&nbsp;
opposed to some large European bank. Eventually,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.360 --> 00:03:51.040
the company would grow so large that they would&nbsp;
actually buy out Ireland's second biggest health&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.040 --> 00:03:57.440
insurance provider, Bupa Ireland in 2007 for&nbsp;
150 million euros. This was a pretty big bet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.440 --> 00:04:03.360
but Sean was easily able to afford it. After all,&nbsp;
his finance company had turned over 123 million&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.360 --> 00:04:09.120
euros worth of profit within the first 6 months of&nbsp;
2006. Aside from the financial industry, Sean also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.120 --> 00:04:14.000
dove into the hospitality industry. Throughout&nbsp;
the 1990s, Sean purchased a plethora of hotels&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.000 --> 00:04:20.080
across Ireland, the UK, and Eastern Europe. One of&nbsp;
his most famous acquisitions came in 2004 when he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.080 --> 00:04:25.440
purchased the Prague Hilton for 145 million euros.&nbsp;
Sean didn’t let all these new investments distract&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.440 --> 00:04:30.000
him from growing his core business either. In the&nbsp;
2000s, he was actively expanding the construction&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:35.840
business into chemicals, radiators, and energy. At&nbsp;
this point, Sean’s business was definitely way too&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.840 --> 00:04:42.240
big and way too diversified to fail, but it only&nbsp;
took one bad investment to erase decades of work.

00:04:42.240 --> 00:04:46.000
ONE BAD INVESTMENT:&nbsp;

00:04:46.000 --> 00:04:50.960
The bulk of Sean’s financial pain circles back to&nbsp;
a fateful investment into the Anglo Irish Bank.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.960 --> 00:04:54.960
Looking back, this bank was notorious for&nbsp;
funding rich property developers with money&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.960 --> 00:05:00.640
that they would gamble on speculative real estate&nbsp;
plays. In retrospect, it’s extremely obvious why&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.640 --> 00:05:04.880
you wouldn’t want to invest in such a company&nbsp;
during the housing bubble, but at the time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.880 --> 00:05:10.080
it was much less obvious. From Sean’s perspective,&nbsp;
the bank had relationships with a bunch of wealthy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.080 --> 00:05:14.800
real estate developers, so it seemed like a&nbsp;
pretty safe bet. Throughout the early 2000s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.800 --> 00:05:20.560
Sean invested a total of $1.3 billion into the&nbsp;
bank growing a 28% stake. If this investment were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.560 --> 00:05:26.000
to collapse, it would already be quite painful,&nbsp;
but Sean wouldn’t be in risk of bankruptcy. This,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.000 --> 00:05:31.120
however, would change when the stock of the&nbsp;
bank started to drop in late 2007. You see, Sean&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.120 --> 00:05:35.920
thought that this was just a great buy the dip&nbsp;
opportunity and that it was just a matter of time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.920 --> 00:05:40.720
until the bank recovered. So, Sean doubled down&nbsp;
on his bet, and took out loans against his other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.720 --> 00:05:45.840
companies to buy Contract for Differences or CFDs.&nbsp;
CFDs are a type of financial derivative and it’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.840 --> 00:05:51.360
inner workings are quite complex, but all you need&nbsp;
to know is that it’s a form of leveraged trading.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.360 --> 00:05:55.680
This means that gains and losses in share&nbsp;
price are amplified. For example, if the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.680 --> 00:06:02.080
share price recovers 2% you might make 20%, but&nbsp;
the problem is that if it drops 2%, you lose 20%.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.960 --> 00:06:07.760
Now, there’s no excuse for borrowing money to&nbsp;
make such a gamble, but Sean wasn’t an idiot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.760 --> 00:06:12.240
either. There were a couple of big misleading&nbsp;
green flags that led him down this path.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.240 --> 00:06:16.400
First of all, Anglo stock was performing way&nbsp;
better than the rest of the banking industry.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.400 --> 00:06:20.960
This doesn’t mean that Anglo stock was at all&nbsp;
time highs, but it was down less than the overall&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.960 --> 00:06:26.240
financial industry. This made Sean confident that&nbsp;
other investors also saw the potential in Anglo,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.240 --> 00:06:30.240
but the reality was that a dozen extremely wealthy&nbsp;
individuals were propping up the share price.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.800 --> 00:06:35.120
Once Anglo stock started to fall, Anglo allegedly&nbsp;
reached out to it’s top 10 biggest investors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.120 --> 00:06:40.160
and directors and gave them loans to buy Anglo&nbsp;
stock. Apparently, this is why Sean himself was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.160 --> 00:06:44.960
buying Anglo stock. So, all this extra buying&nbsp;
pressure created a false sense of security.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.960 --> 00:06:49.120
Aside from propping up the stock price, Anglo was&nbsp;
also allegedly propping up their fundamentals.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.120 --> 00:06:53.200
Anglo had loaned out money to one of their&nbsp;
rivals who then turned around and deposited the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.200 --> 00:06:58.080
money back into Anglo which made it seem like&nbsp;
Anglo was more solvent than they really were.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.080 --> 00:07:01.600
This charade helped Anglo stay afloat through&nbsp;
the beginning of the financial crisis,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.600 --> 00:07:05.120
but as other financial institutions&nbsp;
started to collapse, Anglo would collapse&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.120 --> 00:07:09.440
as well. And Sean who was basically&nbsp;
all in on Anglo got obliterated.

00:07:09.440 --> 00:07:13.120
COURT BATTLE:&nbsp;

00:07:13.120 --> 00:07:18.000
Given that Sean had lost it all on Anglo stock, he&nbsp;
was no longer able to pay back his loans to Anglo,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.000 --> 00:07:23.040
and Anglo would come after Sean. Sean and his&nbsp;
family have fired back with lawsuits against Anglo&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.040 --> 00:07:26.400
claiming that they basically propped&nbsp;
up the stock price and fundamentals.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.400 --> 00:07:29.440
It was never really determined if&nbsp;
these allegations are true or not,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.440 --> 00:07:34.880
but here’s what we know. Anglo did in fact give&nbsp;
out loans to top investors, directors, and a rival&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.880 --> 00:07:40.000
who in turn invested back into Anglo. But Anglo&nbsp;
claims that there were no laws being broken. They&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.000 --> 00:07:44.640
were simply fulfilling their roles as a bank by&nbsp;
loaning out money and giving out financial advice.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.640 --> 00:07:48.240
It’s not like they were committing&nbsp;
securities fraud or running a pump and dump.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.240 --> 00:07:53.280
Also, it wasn’t the bank’s fault that Sean went&nbsp;
all in on Anglo stock. He was clearly smart enough&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.280 --> 00:07:58.000
in the 1990s to diversify away from construction.&nbsp;
He should’ve known not to put all his eggs in one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.000 --> 00:08:02.400
basket regardless of how confident he was in&nbsp;
the investment. With not enough money left to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.400 --> 00:08:06.720
run his businesses, Sean would voluntarily&nbsp;
file for bankruptcy in Northern Ireland,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.720 --> 00:08:11.760
but even this would end poorly. You see, Sean’s&nbsp;
choice to file for bankruptcy in Northern Ireland&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.760 --> 00:08:16.160
was strategic because the law in Northern Ireland&nbsp;
allows bankrupt businesses to return to business&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.160 --> 00:08:21.040
after 12 months. However, a judge would overturn&nbsp;
this bankruptcy filing asserting that Sean’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.040 --> 00:08:25.840
main businesses were run out of Ireland, not just&nbsp;
Northern Ireland. This made it impossible for Sean&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.840 --> 00:08:30.160
to restart his business without paying back his&nbsp;
loans, but without his businesses, there was no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.160 --> 00:08:35.520
chance that he could ever pay them back. So, Sean&nbsp;
was stuck in a catch 22 situation. This went on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.520 --> 00:08:40.400
until Ireland decided to nationalize Anglo Irish&nbsp;
Bank and bail them out with 34 billion euros.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.400 --> 00:08:44.880
Once the bank was nationalized, it wasn’t just&nbsp;
the bank who wanted Sean to repay his loans,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.880 --> 00:08:48.560
but it was the state and they were looking&nbsp;
to take all of his remaining assets.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.560 --> 00:08:53.040
Seeing this, Sean and his kids would try to&nbsp;
stowaway as many assets as they could offshore.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.040 --> 00:08:57.680
But, they would quickly be caught and charged&nbsp;
resulting in Sean being sent to jail for 9 weeks.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.680 --> 00:09:02.000
And with that, Sean had officially fallen from&nbsp;
being Ireland’s richest man to being a convict.

00:09:02.000 --> 00:09:05.680
SEAN TODAY:&nbsp;

00:09:05.680 --> 00:09:10.320
All these lawsuits and court battles have been&nbsp;
extremely expensive for both Sean and the state.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.320 --> 00:09:16.240
Apparently, the battle cost the state a total&nbsp;
of 170 million euros as of 2019, so they finally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.240 --> 00:09:21.200
decided to settle the case. The court has agreed&nbsp;
to let the Quinn family keep 440 million euros&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.200 --> 00:09:25.760
if they liquidate their remaining assets and pay&nbsp;
back at least 500 million euros to the state. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.760 --> 00:09:29.760
it looks like the Quinn family will end up walking&nbsp;
away with a large sum of money nonetheless.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.760 --> 00:09:34.720
This has left a lot of the public divided. A lot&nbsp;
of people in Ireland still strongly support Sean&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.720 --> 00:09:39.200
suggesting that Sean made a calculated risk on&nbsp;
Anglo, and if it wasn’t for Anglo’s deception,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.200 --> 00:09:44.000
Sean’s investment would’ve panned out. They feel&nbsp;
that Ireland unfairly took sides with Anglo,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.000 --> 00:09:48.480
and that Sean is innocent. On the other side&nbsp;
of the spectrum, critics argue that it doesn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.480 --> 00:09:53.760
matter whether Anglo was deceptive or not. Sean’s&nbsp;
leveraged investment was extremely irresponsible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.760 --> 00:09:58.000
and he’s simply facing the consequences of his&nbsp;
actions. Not to mention, it was the people who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.000 --> 00:10:02.400
trusted Sean and bought his insurance policies&nbsp;
that sustained the most impactful losses.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.400 --> 00:10:06.800
In the meantime, it looks like Sean is&nbsp;
still walking away with 440 million euros.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.800 --> 00:10:10.720
Critics also point to Sean’s instinct to hide&nbsp;
away his assets as a glimpse at his true colors.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.280 --> 00:10:15.440
At the end of the day, Sean started off as&nbsp;
a poor farm boy who used his ingenuity and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.440 --> 00:10:19.680
humbleness to reach unthinkable heights. At&nbsp;
one point, he was cherished by the Irish and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.680 --> 00:10:24.320
regarded as a miraculous success story. But,&nbsp;
it only took one overconfident investment to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.320 --> 00:10:29.520
destroy the empire and reputation that he had so&nbsp;
painstakingly built. Who do you think is to blame,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:29.520 --> 00:10:34.000
Sean or Anglo? Comment that down below.&nbsp;
Also, drop a like if you think that greed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.000 --> 00:10:37.520
kills. And of course, consider joining&nbsp;
